  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below average temperatures and mainly dry\nconditions are expected into the weekend. \n\nSurface high pressure, originating from Canada, remains overhead\ntoday before slowly shifting towards New England. Below normal\ntemperatures are expected today and tomorrow with highs in the\n70s to low 80s. Southerly winds bring increased cloud cover\ntoday with an upper level trough yielding a slight chance of\nrain showers in the southern portions of the forecast area.\nPrecipitation chances continue Saturday afternoon, mainly along\nthe I-81 corridor in the afternoon before conditions dry out\novernight. Sunday will be the warmest day of the weekend with \nhighs in the 70s to upper 80s with dry conditions expected. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Returning to a summer-like pattern next week.\n\nComing off of the weekend, an upper-level trough begins to dig into \nthe northeastern U.S. This will usher in the potential for organized \nconvection early to mid week. In spite of multiple frontal passages \nduring the period, next week could carry a return to summer-like \nconditions with heat indices in the mid 90s to near 100 degrees.\n\nThe key players will be a longstanding upper ridge across the \nsouthwestern U.S. into the southern/central Great Plains, a deep \nupper low near Hudson Bay, and longwave trough over the eastern U.S. \nWhile ensemble solutions do not show a more formidable shortwave \ntracking across the area, the setup certainly lends itself to a more \nactive pattern. A series of \"ridge rollers\" are likely to cross the \nnorthern tier of the country before crossing the Great Lakes and \nnortheastern states. At this juncture, the machine-learning \nalgorithms favor an uptick in convection on Monday into Tuesday. \nThis would present the most likely opportunity for any severe \nthunderstorms. A progressive pattern aloft will bring a cold front \nthrough the region on Tuesday evening/night, with a secondary \nboundary on its heels. While temperatures remain seasonable in this \nmid/late week pattern shift, dew points slowly come down to finish \nout the month of July.",
